CHAPTER II PREVENTION OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND CHILDREN Part One Forms of Violence Against Women and Children Article

Forms of violence against women and children include:

a.
c. d.  f. physical violence;

psychological violence;

sexual violence; neglect;

exploitation; and/or other violence.

Article Physical violence as referred to in Article letter constitutes an act that results in pain accompanied by injury, wounds or disability to the body, miscarriage, unconsciousness and/or causes death of women and/or children.

Article Psychological violence as referred to in Article letter constitutes an act that results in fear, loss of self-confidence, loss of ability to act, feelings of helplessness and/or psychological suffering to women and/or children. Article Sexual violence as referred to in Article letter includes:

a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. coercion of unnatural or unwanted sexual relations; and/or c. coercion of sexual relations with others for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes.

Article 23

Prevention as referred to in Article 22 letter a, is implemented by: (1)

a. establishing a network in efforts to prevent violence against women and children;
b. conducting coordination, integration, and synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
c. establishing a system for preventing violence against women and children;
d. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children victims of violence;
e. providing knowledge about prevention and mechanisms for overcoming violence against women and children; and
f. enhancing the role and value of local wisdom and the role of customs in the protection of women and children.

Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in an integrated manner by Regional Apparatus that (2) have main duties and functions in the fields of:

empowerment of women and protection of children; education;

health;

social affairs and manpower;

youth and sports; and
f. mental and spiritual.

Regional Apparatus in preventing acts of (3) violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (1) may involve related agencies.

In addition to being implemented by Regional Apparatus and related agencies as referred to in paragraph (3), prevention (4) is also implemented by:

a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community, NGOs, universities, community organizations, social organizations and religious organizations; and
c. business world.

Procedures for preventing acts of violence against women and (5) children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

Paragraph Services

Article 16

Women and children victims of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 15, also receive special rights, as follows:

a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;

the right to basic services;

the right to equal protection;

the right to be free from various stigma;

the right to obtain freedom;

the right to obtain empowerment; and the right to be tried based on restorative justice.

CHAPTER VII

INVESTIGATION

Article 44

In addition to Investigating Officers of the State Police of the Republic of Indonesia, investigations of criminal acts as referred to in paragraph (1) of this Regional Regulation may be carried out by Civil Servants within the Bengkalis Regency Government whose appointment is determined in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Civil Servant Investigators as referred to in paragraph (2) of paragraph (1) are authorized to:

a. examine the truth of reports or information concerning criminal acts related to violence against women and children;
b. examine any person and/or business entity suspected of participating in criminal acts related to violence against women and children in the business environment;
c. request information from any person and/or business entity in connection with criminal acts related to violence against women and children;
d. examine evidence materials, records, or other documents concerning criminal acts related to violence against women and children;
e. conduct examinations at certain places suspected of containing evidence materials;
f. carry out seizures of goods and materials resulting from violations that can be used as evidence in criminal cases related to violence against women and children;
g. request assistance from experts in the framework of carrying out investigative duties concerning criminal acts related to violence against women and children; and
h. terminate the investigation if there is insufficient evidence of the existence of criminal acts related to violence against women and children.

In carrying out their authority, Civil Servant Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) shall coordinate with the Indonesian National Police in accordance with the provisions of paragraph (3) of laws and regulations.

4. Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) shall notify the commencement of the investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the Public Prosecutor through the Investigator Officer of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia in accordance with the provisions regulated in the Criminal Procedure Code.

Article 41, the Regional Government may partner with the business sector, NGOs/social organizations concerned with women and children in the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out through: (2)

a. notification of employment opportunity information for women victims of violence;
b. education and training for women and children victims of violence;
c. educational assistance for women and children victims of violence who have been withdrawn from their education; d. fostering and increasing the economic independence of women victims of violence;
e. providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities, the private sector and various non-governmental organizations concerned with women and children from various forms of violence; and/org. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from various forms of violence up to the Village level.

Partnership as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) is set forth in the form of an agreement in accordance with (3) the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 10

Neglect as referred to in Article letter includes: a. acts that result in the failure to fulfill the needs of children properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care; b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after children as they should be, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts of deliberately neglecting women and children within their household scope, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to such women; and/or d. acts that result in economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting decent work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

Part Three Rights of Victims

Article 15

Women and children victims of violence receive the following rights: a. the right to have their dignity respected as human beings; b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims; the right to determine their own decisions;

the right to obtain information;

the right to confidentiality especially regarding their identity; the right to compensation;

the right to social rehabilitation;

the right to complaint handling;

the right to assistance; and i.
j. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilities in the judicial process.

CHAPTER III PROVISION OF SERVICES FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE Part One General

Article 17

Protection of women and children is the shared responsibility of:

a. Regional Government;
b. Parents, Guardians and/or Family;
c. Community; andd. Business/private sector.

Article 32

Social rehabilitation as referred to in Article 26 paragraph (1) letter number is a service provided by assistants to restore the traumatic condition of victims.

(1 Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by: (2)

a. providing guidance and counseling;
b. psychological recovery of victims;
c. accompanying victims in the family and community environment; and d. providing Safe Houses for victims of violence who require ongoing treatment.

Special social rehabilitation for women victims of domestic violence and trafficking in persons is carried out by: (3)a. providing guidance and counseling;
b. psychological recovery of victims;
c. accompanying victims in the family and community environment; d. providing skills training and education; and €. providing business capital assistance.

Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented by Regional Apparatus having principal tasks and functions in the fields of: (4)

a. social affairs;
b. Cc.  d. women's empowerment and child protection; education health; ande. mental and spiritual.

In addition to being implemented by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (4), social rehabilitation may also be implemented by the community or social service (5) institutions.

REGENT OF BENGKALIS,

Considering/In view of the fact that women and children have the right to obtain a sense of

a. security and freedom from acts of violence, exploitation and maltreatment as guaranteed in the 1945 Constitution;

that the implementation of protection of women and children is the authority of the Regional Government based on Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government; that in order to prevent and protect

c. women and children victims of violence, regulations are needed to guarantee efforts to implement the provision of protection of women and children in the Region; that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection of Women and Children;

